13. Constraints, assumptions, dependencies and risks
Constraints
•	PIMS remains the master database. Fabric is where data is improved and prepared; it is not a new system for end users.
•	Survey and certificate formats can't be standardised or changed after the fact. They are legal documents, and the business has limited influence over contractor templates.
•	WhereScape is switched off early in 2027.
Assumptions
•	The standardised column headers from the Asbestos & Compliance Lead are the agreed target structure.
•	Whichever front end is chosen can support search, filters, colour coding and document links.
•	Survey contractors will continue to supply survey data in a consistent structure that can be loaded automatically.
Dependencies
•	The Asbestos & Compliance Lead defining the conflict and precedence rules (RULE-04) before the consolidation logic is built.
•	The data cleansing and reconciliation already under way.
•	The decision on which front end will show the register.
•	Possible existing contractor portal features for requester notifications.
Risks
Risk	Impact	Suggested owner
Consolidation rules wrongly hide a positive item	High: health and safety, and legal exposure	Asbestos & Compliance Lead
Automation skips review of observations noted on surveys or consignment notes	Medium to high: site information missed	Responsible person for asbestos
Stakeholders unclear about Fabric versus PIMS, which affects buy-in	Medium	Innovation Project Manager
Historic naming can't be fully mapped	Medium: duplicates remain	Workstream 1 engineers
Scope creep into handhelds, training and survey programmes	Medium	Business Analyst
